<configure
    xmlns="http://namespaces.zope.org/zope"
    xmlns:browser="http://namespaces.zope.org/browser"
    i18n_domain="imio.emailkit"
    >

  <!-- The raster assets the kit shell and `KitPill` reference by absolute URL:
       the v3 head artwork (`art-head.png` with its `art-head-tail.png`), the
       four pill icons, the two iMio logos and the Quicksand weights `fonts.css`
       serves.

       The artwork PNGs are crops of the design's own SVG sources, converted to PNG
       and kept transparent so the card shows through them and an image-blocking
       client is left with a clean flat rather than a hole. They are the one place
       a consumer can rebrand the top of the mail: `primary_color` drives
       `KitCard`'s rail and `KitButton`'s fill but cannot recolour a raster, so a
       site that needs its own artwork replaces these two files and touches no
       markup.

       Mail clients fetch every image over HTTP, so a kit image cannot be a file
       inside the egg the way a `.pt` is; it has to be a URL the recipient's
       client can reach while reading the message, days later, logged out. A
       browser resource directory is the one thing in Plone that is exactly that:
       a stable, anonymously readable, cacheable URL under the portal.

       Registered on IDefaultBrowserLayer rather than IEmailkitLayer. A `:base`
       site (the opt-out) keeps stock Plone's default mails but still
       renders its OWN templates through the kit shell, and those reference the
       same assets; binding the directory to the layer would give that site a
       footer logo that 404s.

       PNG and not SVG, at twice the rendered size. Mail clients do not display
       SVG reliably, and the 2x source is what keeps the 14 px pills and the
       68 px footer logo sharp on a retina screen.

       The shell composes the URL as `${portal_url}/++resource++imio.emailkit/...`
       and skips the image entirely when `portal_url` is empty, which is what a
       render outside a request (a golden file, a unit test) produces. A relative
       `/++resource++...` in an inbox is a broken image icon, so degrading to no
       image is the deliberate choice. -->
  <browser:resourceDirectory
      name="imio.emailkit"
      directory="static"
      />

  <!-- z3c.jbot, for the promise that a site can override the `.pt` files
       THIS package resolves. No `browser:jbot` directive any more, since this
       package overrides no stock Plone template now that `default_mails.py` has
       taken the two views over; but the include is still load-bearing, and in
       the less obvious direction.

       The WHOLE package, never `file="meta.zcml"` (Phase 0 caveat D1).
       `meta.zcml` registers only the `browser:jbot` directive; the
       `ViewPageTemplateFile.__get__` monkeypatches live in
       `z3c.jbot/configure.zcml`. Those patches are what `render()._page_template`
       invokes by hand so a consumer's override of one of our templates is picked
       up, and what keeps stock Plone's `login_help.pt` overridable where
       `login_help.py` reuses it by absolute path. Without them there is no error
       and no warning; overrides are simply ignored.

       CMFPlone does not include z3c.jbot itself. In a real instance
       z3c.autoinclude pulls it in, but PLONE_FIXTURE disables autoinclude, so
       the include has to be here and test layers must not rely on autoinclude.
  -->
  <include package="z3c.jbot" />

  <!-- The two remaining Plone default mails, as owned views.

       They were `z3c.jbot` overrides of stock Plone's page templates until
       `browser/default_mails.py` took the views over. jbot could reach them, so
       it was used; the cost was that a stock view rendered them, which meant a
       foreign dialect, no locale helpers, no `render()` and therefore no
       discovery, no preview and no golden files. Owning the view makes them
       ordinary templates, exactly as it already did for `get_username` below.

       Same `name`, same `for` and same `permission` as the stock registrations
       in `Products/CMFPlone/browser/login/configure.zcml`, differing only by
       layer. No `template=`: the classes render a registered template through
       `render()` and return the RFC822 document `RegistrationTool` expects.

       On IEmailkitLayer, which only the `:default` profile installs. That
       binding IS the opt-out: a `:base` site keeps stock Plone's views
       and stock Plone's mails. -->
  <browser:page
      name="mail_password_template"
      for="*"
      class=".default_mails.MailPasswordView"
      permission="zope.Public"
      layer="imio.emailkit.interfaces.IEmailkitLayer"
      />

  <browser:page
      name="registered_notify_template"
      for="*"
      class=".default_mails.RegisteredNotifyView"
      permission="zope.Public"
      layer="imio.emailkit.interfaces.IEmailkitLayer"
      />

  <!-- The third restyled Plone default mail: the username reminder from the
       login-help form.

       Stock Plone has no template for it. It is `SEND_USERNAME_TEMPLATE`, a
       module-level i18n string in `Products.CMFPlone.browser.login.login_help`,
       declared text/plain and handed straight to MailHost by
       `RequestUsername.send_username()`. z3c.jbot keys on a resolved filename and
       there is no file, so the view is the only seam.

       Same `name`, same `for` and same `permission` as the stock registration in
       `Products/CMFPlone/browser/login/configure.zcml`, differing only by layer.
       No `template=`: the class supplies `index` itself, pointing at Plone's own
       login_help.pt by absolute path, so the form markup is not forked and stays
       jbot-overridable.

       On IEmailkitLayer, exactly like the two views above, so the opt-out
       covers this mail too: a `:base` site gets stock Plone's view and stock
       Plone's plaintext mail. -->
  <browser:page
      name="login-help"
      for="plone.base.interfaces.INavigationRoot"
      class=".login_help.LoginHelpForm"
      permission="zope.Public"
      layer="imio.emailkit.interfaces.IEmailkitLayer"
      />

  <!-- The kit layout's last resort for the theme tokens, for a consumer whose
       template is rendered by a stock view rather than by `render()`: neither
       `theme` nor `options/theme` is in scope there. No template this package
       ships needs it any more, now that it renders all three of its default
       mails itself, but the layout keeps the fallback. Registered on
       IDefaultBrowserLayer rather than IEmailkitLayer so a `:base` consumer
       rendering its own templates through a stock view has the same escape.

       `get_username` does not need it: its own view renders it through
       `render()`, which injects `theme` into the flat context directly. -->
  <browser:page
      name="emailkit_theme"
      for="*"
      class=".theme.EmailkitThemeView"
      permission="zope2.View"
      />

  <!-- The preview. Two registrations, one feature: the page, and the
       rendered mail on its own for the page's <iframe>. Splitting them is what
       keeps the mail's stylesheet (written for mail clients, full of `body {}`
       rules and !important utilities) out of the Plone UI and vice versa.

       `cmf.ManagePortal`, not `zope2.View`: this renders arbitrary registered
       templates against arbitrary committed fixture data and has a button that
       puts mail on the wire. Anonymous and ordinary members get Unauthorized.

       On IEmailkitLayer, so the preview only exists where the restyled mails do:
       a preview that showed stock Plone's markup would be lying about the very
       thing it exists to show. Consequence, and it is deliberate: a site that
       installed `:base` for the opt-out has no preview view. That is the
       opposite call from `emailkit_theme` above, which a `:base` site genuinely
       needs at render time; nothing renders because of this one.

       (No double hyphen anywhere in this comment: XML forbids it, and ZCML then
       fails to parse at instance start-up.) -->

  <!-- `cmf.ManagePortal` has to be a REGISTERED permission utility by the time
       the two `browser:page` actions below execute, and ZCML executes actions in
       the order they were collected. cookiecutter-zope-instance writes a
       site.zcml that includes this package BEFORE `<five:loadProducts />`, so
       Products.CMFCore's permissions.zcml has not run yet and the registrations
       below die with
       `ComponentLookupError: (IPermission, 'cmf.ManagePortal')` at start-up.

       Including it here is idempotent (zope.configuration skips an already
       processed file), so this only ever moves the declaration earlier. Same
       reasoning, and the same placement, as the z3c.jbot include at the top: the
       include sits next to the directive that needs it, where the mistake would
       otherwise be made. -->
  <include
      package="Products.CMFCore"
      file="permissions.zcml"
      />

  <browser:page
      name="emailkit-preview"
      for="*"
      class=".preview.EmailkitPreview"
      permission="cmf.ManagePortal"
      layer="imio.emailkit.interfaces.IEmailkitLayer"
      />

  <browser:page
      name="emailkit-preview-body"
      for="*"
      class=".preview.EmailkitPreviewBody"
      permission="cmf.ManagePortal"
      layer="imio.emailkit.interfaces.IEmailkitLayer"
      />

</configure>
